# Cat, the live loop (the button's startup script): a little cat on the Mac's screen that chases the pointer.
#
# The tap (the button's command) writes the second the cat goes home by itself to $dir/until, or removes the file
# to call the cat home now. At rest this loop only looks at that file four times a second (a builtin read, no
# process started). While the file says the cat is out, the loop keeps the overlay running: one osascript (JXA)
# process that draws the cat over every display (its own notes are below). The loop keeps $dir/out while it wants
# the cat; once that file is gone the cat runs off the screen and the overlay quits. The overlay also quits when
# this loop is gone, even killed (its parent changes), and this loop ends it at once on TERM, INT and HUP: the
# agent stops startup scripts when the phone disconnects and when it quits.
#
# The button keeps its own face (a cat, "Cat") at rest; while the cat is out, the same cat in orange. That is two
# posts a visit, each one curl call that runs beside this loop (a slow agent never holds it up); nothing is posted
# while nothing changes.
#
# The cat itself is drawn as text right below (cat_palette, cat_pictures): change a colour or a pixel there. What
# it does and how fast is at the top of the overlay (cat_js). To look at the result without the phone:
#   zsh startup.zsh --sheet cat.png  every picture facing right and left, magnified, into cat.png (no window)
#   zsh startup.zsh --sim            the cat chasing a made-up pointer over two made-up displays, a line per change
#   CAT_DRY=1 zsh startup.zsh        the loop as the agent runs it, but posts to the button are printed, not sent;
#                                    with CAT_GHOST=1 too the cat is not drawn either: what it does is printed.
#                                    Let it out with tap.zsh and the same DESMAPTIC_STORAGE.
emulate -R zsh                                  # zsh's own options, whatever ~/.zshenv set
export LC_ALL=C
export PATH="/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"

# ---- The cat --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A picture is 64 pixels wide and 48 high, one letter per pixel; "." is see-through. The cat stands on the bottom
# row and faces right: the cat facing left is the mirror image. Every picture starts with "= name", and the names
# are what the overlay asks for (sit, blink, alert, run1-3, wash1-2, scratch1-2, yawn1-2, sleep1-2, and z: the
# letter that floats up from a sleeping cat), so keep them. A letter needs a colour in the palette: the letter, then
# red green blue in hex. One pixel is 1 pt on the screen (PT in cat_js). A ginger cat: k 2B1D16, l FFBF75,
# o F5A04A, s E3863A, d C9662A, c FFF1DC, e EBDBC4.
cat_palette='
k  2A2B31   outline
l  CBCCD2   fur in the light
o  B3B4BB   fur, light grey
s  9B9CA4   fur in the shade, the far legs
d  7D7E87   stripes
c  F4EEE6   cream: muzzle, chin, chest, paws
e  DCD4CB   cream in the shade
a  EDCFBB   peach: the bridge of the nose, round the nose
p  F4A6B0   pink: ears, nose, tongue
r  A83245   inside the mouth
g  BFC95A   eyes, yellow-green
w  FFFFFF   white: the shine in the eyes, the z of a sleeping cat
'

cat_pictures='
